{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Foundations of Interactive Multimedia Learning", "title": "Cognitive Load Diagnosis", "description": "AIL 606 textbook section connecting cognitive load diagnosis to cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence shows a defensible decision in AIL 606.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next."], "concept_ids": ["ail606_supplement_foundations_of_interactive_multimedia_learning", "cognitive_load", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 3, "top_tier_hardened": false, "course": "ail606-supplement", "chapter": "01", "section": "03", "prereq_section_ids": ["ail606-supplement/01/02", "inst-design/02/06"], "practice_ids": ["ail606_supplement_1_3_cognitive_load_diagnosis_artifact_signal", "ail606_supplement_1_3_cognitive_load_diagnosis_annotation_signal", "ail606_supplement_1_3_cognitive_load_diagnosis_ai_boundary", "ail606_supplement_1_3_cognitive_load_diagnosis_calibration"]}, "content": "# Cognitive Load Diagnosis\n\n![Cognitive Load Diagnosis textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/01-03-cognitive-load-diagnosis.png)\n\n<youtube-embed id=\"KbzmM30NXNQ\" title=\"Cognitive Load Theory: An Introduction\" caption=\"Use this short overview as a check on your diagnosis: name the load source first, then revise the artifact rather than making it merely cleaner.\"></youtube-embed>\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe previous sections gave you the architecture and the principles. This section is about the clinical skill that uses them: diagnosis. A doctor who can only say \"the patient feels unwell\" cannot prescribe; a designer who can only say \"the lesson is too hard\" cannot revise. Diagnosis means decomposing a single felt difficulty into its three sources, intrinsic, extraneous, and germane, and attributing each to something in the artifact a reviewer could point at. The diagnosis table is the chart. Each row is a differential diagnosis: this much of the difficulty is in the content, this much in the presentation, this much is the productive struggle we must not remove.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Decompose a single difficulty into intrinsic, extraneous, and germane components.\n- Estimate element interactivity for a named learner and tie it to a revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to decide what help or design change should come next.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA pilot tester says a statistics tutorial on interaction effects is \"too hard,\" and the first instinct is to cut content until it feels easy. But \"too hard\" is a symptom with at least three possible causes, and the wrong cut deletes the very concept being taught. The diagnostic question is not \"how do I make this easier?\" It is \"of the difficulty this learner feels, how much is the inherent complexity of an interaction effect, how much is a confusing rainbow legend, and how much is the desirable effort of predicting a missing cell?\"\n\n## Core Concept\n\nTotal cognitive load is additive across the three sources and bounded by working-memory capacity. Informally,\n\n$$ L_{total} = L_{intrinsic} + L_{extraneous} + L_{germane} \\le C_{wm} $$\n\nThe design implication follows directly from the inequality. You cannot raise germane processing (the load that builds the schema) unless there is capacity to spare, and you free capacity mainly by cutting extraneous load, because intrinsic load is fixed by the content for a given learner. So the diagnostic order is forced: cut extraneous first, then sequence intrinsic, then spend the freed capacity on germane effort.\n\nManipulate the three loads against a fixed capacity and watch the spare capacity available for schema-building. Try cutting extraneous to zero, then see how much germane effort you can add before the total exceeds capacity.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Additive load against working-memory capacity\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"intrinsic\",\"label\":\"Intrinsic load (content for this learner)\",\"min\":1,\"max\":7,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"el\",\"default\":4},{\"name\":\"extraneous\",\"label\":\"Extraneous load (presentation waste)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":6,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"el\",\"default\":3},{\"name\":\"germane\",\"label\":\"Germane load (schema-building effort)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":6,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"el\",\"default\":1},{\"name\":\"capacity\",\"label\":\"Working-memory capacity\",\"min\":4,\"max\":9,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"el\",\"default\":7}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Total load\",\"unit\":\"el\",\"expr\":\"intrinsic + extraneous + germane\"},{\"label\":\"Spare capacity (negative = overload)\",\"unit\":\"el\",\"expr\":\"capacity - (intrinsic + extraneous + germane)\"},{\"label\":\"Germane room freed by cutting all extraneous\",\"unit\":\"el\",\"expr\":\"capacity - intrinsic - germane\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"extraneous\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nIntrinsic load itself is governed by **element interactivity**, the number of elements that must be held and related simultaneously because they cannot be understood one at a time. A high element-interactivity task for a novice can be low for an expert, because the expert's schema chunks several elements into one. That is why every diagnosis row must name the learner.\n\n| Source | Diagnostic question | Legitimate remedy |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Intrinsic | Must these elements be processed together? For whom? | Pre-train, segment, sequence |\n| Extraneous | Does the presentation add work unrelated to the goal? | Remove or restructure |\n| Germane | Is this effort building the target schema? | Protect; do not optimize away |\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA first draft hides the real decision behind a vague claim:\n\n> I made the cognitive load diagnosis table clearer.\n\nA diagnostic entry separates the three sources and names a remedy for each:\n\n> For a novice who knows main effects but not interactions, the intrinsic load is high (two factors plus their joint effect held at once). The rainbow legend adds extraneous scanning. The predict-the-cell prompt is germane. Remedy: pre-train on a single main effect (intrinsic), label lines on the plot and drop the legend (extraneous), keep the prediction (germane).\n\nThe improvement is that each entry names which load it is and what evidence places it there, so a reviewer can challenge a specific cell rather than the whole lesson.\n\n### Annotated Before/After: A Three-Column Diagnosis\n\nHere is one row of a cognitive-load diagnosis table for a graduate statistics tutorial on interaction effects, revised so each load is correctly sourced.\n\n**Before (single column).** The table has one note: \"This lesson is too hard. Make it easier.\" Target learner: unspecified.\n\nThe problem is that \"too hard\" merges three different loads, so no specific fix follows.\n\n**After (three columns, with the source named).** Target learner: a novice who knows main effects but has not met interactions.\n\n1. Intrinsic load (high): an interaction requires holding two factors and their joint effect at once, which is high element interactivity for this learner. Decision: add pre-training on reading a single main effect before the interaction plot appears.\n2. Extraneous load (present): the original plot used a rainbow legend off to the side, forcing back-and-forth scanning. Decision: label the two lines directly on the plot (spatial contiguity) and drop the legend.\n3. Germane load (to protect): the learner is asked to predict the missing cell before it is revealed. Decision: keep this self-explanation prompt; it is the processing that builds the schema.\n\n**Rejected suggestion.** An AI tutor offered to \"simplify by removing the second factor.\" Rejected: removing a factor deletes the very concept being taught. The defensible move manages intrinsic load through sequencing, not by cutting essential content.\n\nThe improvement is that each entry now names which load it is and what evidence places it there, so the next reviewer can challenge a specific cell rather than the whole lesson.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"The additivity inequality says total load is bounded by capacity. Using your own words, explain why the order must be cut extraneous first, then sequence intrinsic, then add germane, rather than starting by adding germane prompts.\" answer=\"Intrinsic load is fixed by the content for a given learner and germane effort only pays off if there is spare capacity. Extraneous load is the only term you can remove at no cost to the concept, so cutting it first frees the most room. Sequencing intrinsic (pre-training, segmenting) lowers the fixed term without deleting content. Only then is there headroom to spend on germane prompts. Adding germane first risks pushing total load over capacity while extraneous waste is still consuming the budget.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDiagnose in the order the additivity inequality dictates:\n\n1. **Cut extraneous first.** It is free capacity with no cost to the content. Split attention, redundancy, and decorative clutter go here.\n2. **Sequence intrinsic second.** If element interactivity is too high for the learner, lower it by pre-training and segmenting, never by deleting the concept.\n3. **Spend the freed capacity on germane third.** Add or protect the prediction, self-explanation, or comparison that builds the schema.\n\nA revision that lowers a load without saying which of the three it lowered is not a diagnosis; it is a guess.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating \"easier\" as the goal. The goal is the *right* total load for the learner, which sometimes means adding germane effort, not removing it. The second is letting a tool optimize the surface (recolor the plot, smooth the prose) while the intrinsic source is never named, so the lesson still fails for the same reason.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A learner is at capacity and still failing. The tutor proposes removing the predict-the-missing-cell prompt to lighten the load. Diagnose this move.\" explanation=\"The prediction prompt is germane load: the productive effort that builds the schema. Removing it lowers total load but also lowers learning. The right move is to cut extraneous load (the rainbow legend) first, freeing capacity so the germane prompt can stay.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Good move, because any reduction in load helps a saturated learner\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Bad move, it strips germane load; cut the extraneous legend instead to free capacity\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Good move, because prediction prompts are always extraneous\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n The third is forgetting the learner: a row that does not name who the learner is cannot estimate element interactivity and therefore cannot diagnose intrinsic load at all.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTake the \"too hard\" complaint and run the differential. First ask the extraneous question: is anything in the presentation adding work unrelated to interactions? Yes, the rainbow legend forces eye travel between line and label, so that is extraneous and gets cut by moving labels onto the lines. Now ask the intrinsic question: is the remaining difficulty in the concept itself? Yes, an interaction is irreducibly two factors and their product, high element interactivity for a novice, so you sequence it with pre-training rather than deleting a factor. Finally ask the germane question: is any of the difficulty productive? The predict-the-cell prompt is, so you protect it. One symptom, three sources, three different remedies. That is what diagnosis buys you over \"make it easier.\"\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead every difficulty claim as a symptom awaiting decomposition. A strong reader refuses to accept \"hard\" or \"confusing\" as a diagnosis and instead writes three lines, one per source, each tied to something in the artifact and to a named learner. Keep a two-column note: the felt difficulty on the left, its decomposed sources and remedies on the right.\n\nA strong reader of cognitive load diagnosis can answer four questions:\n\n1. Of this difficulty, how much is intrinsic, extraneous, and germane?\n2. Who is the learner, and what does that do to the element-interactivity estimate?\n3. Which remedy fits each source, and which remedies would destroy the concept?\n4. Which difficulty here is productive and must be protected?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe table is strong when each row attributes a portion of the difficulty to a named source with artifact evidence and assigns a source-appropriate remedy. \"Made it easier\" fails; \"extraneous scanning from the legend, fixed by on-plot labels; intrinsic interactivity managed by pre-training; germane prediction kept\" passes. If a row names a load without evidence, mark the gap and ask BigAL to contrast a novice and an expert estimate of element interactivity for that content rather than to restate the definitions.\n\nA useful final entry would read: \"I cut the legend (extraneous), pre-trained the single main effect (intrinsic), and kept the cell-prediction prompt (germane). I rejected deleting the second factor because it removes the concept rather than managing its load.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nHand the same tutorial to a doctoral student who already reads interaction plots fluently. The intrinsic load drops sharply, because their schema chunks the two factors and the joint effect into one element, so pre-training that helped the novice now wastes their time and becomes extraneous. The prediction prompt may also be too easy to be germane and could be replaced with a harder transfer item. The three-source framework is unchanged; the estimates move because the learner moved. If your diagnosis named the learner, you can re-run it; if it only said \"too hard,\" you cannot.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/01-03-cognitive-load-diagnosis-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"01.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the cognitive load diagnosis table with intrinsic/extraneous/germane load evidence for AIL 606 01.03. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_cognitive_load_diagnosis\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that each row names the learner, decomposes the difficulty into intrinsic, extraneous, and germane with artifact evidence, assigns a source-appropriate remedy, and records any suggestion you rejected and why.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nLink the three-source load model to the evidence-based revision it drives and the bounded support an AI tutor can offer.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cognitive_load\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_1_3_cognitive_load_diagnosis_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cognitive_load", "stem": "On a diagnosis table, a designer logs that a lesson on balancing chemical equations is hard 'because the steps interact and cannot be understood one at a time.' Which load category does this describe?", "options": ["Extraneous load caused by poor layout.", "Intrinsic load from high element interactivity in the task itself.", "Germane load from the learner building a schema.", "Extraneous load from a distracting background image."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Intrinsic load comes from the inherent element interactivity of the material. When elements must be processed together to be understood, intrinsic load is high regardless of the layout.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_3_clt_intrinsic_is_fixed_difficulty", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cognitive_load"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_3_cognitive_load_diagnosis_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cognitive_load", "stem": "Two diagnosis-table rows describe the same lesson. Row A: 'cluttered slide with unrelated clip art.' Row B: 'learner self-explains why each step follows.' Which row is extraneous load and which is germane load?", "options": ["Both are extraneous load.", "Row A is extraneous load to reduce; Row B is germane load to support.", "Row A is germane load; Row B is extraneous load.", "Both are intrinsic load and cannot be changed."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Extraneous load is processing wasted on poor design (clip art) and should be cut. Germane load is processing devoted to building schemas (self-explanation) and should be supported.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_3_clt_all_load_is_bad", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cognitive_load"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_3_cognitive_load_diagnosis_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cognitive_load", "stem": "A novice and an expert both work the same dense diagram. The diagnosis table records 'high intrinsic load.' Why must the table state the learner, not just the material?", "options": ["Because intrinsic load is identical for everyone who sees the material.", "Because intrinsic load depends on element interactivity relative to the learner's existing schemas, so the expert's chunked schema lowers it while the novice's load stays high.", "Because experts always experience higher load than novices.", "Because intrinsic load is only about screen layout, not the learner."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Element interactivity is relative to what the learner already knows. An expert's schemas chunk many elements into one, lowering intrinsic load, so a diagnosis must name the target learner.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_3_clt_intrinsic_is_fixed_difficulty", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["cognitive_load"]}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_3_cognitive_load_diagnosis_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "evidence_based_revision", "stem": "A designer wants to lower intrinsic load on a genuinely complex procedure without dumbing it down. Which move is best justified by cognitive load theory?", "options": ["Delete half the steps so the procedure is shorter.", "Use sequencing and pre-training: teach the component concepts first, then present the whole procedure, so fewer novel elements interact at once.", "Add decorative animation to make the procedure feel easier.", "Present every step simultaneously so the learner sees the whole picture at once."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Pre-training and segmenting manage intrinsic load by building partial schemas first, reducing the number of novel interacting elements processed at once, without removing essential content.", "misconception_id": "ail606_supplement_1_3_clt_reduce_load_means_remove_content", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "9e9bd2a95a42582c068ed8ffb85aaddab46e23a54f549e6d7628400982367f6e", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "ail606_supplement_1_3_clt_all_load_is_bad", "pattern": "all_load_is_bad", "description": "Treating every kind of cognitive load as something to minimize, including the germane processing that builds schemas.", "trigger": "learner proposes cutting self-explanation, practice, or elaboration to reduce load", "feedback": "Distinguish the three loads. Cut extraneous load, manage intrinsic load, but protect and support germane load, the processing that builds the schema.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_3_clt_intrinsic_is_fixed_difficulty", "pattern": "intrinsic_load_is_absolute", "description": "Believing intrinsic load is a fixed property of the material that is identical for every learner, ignoring that element interactivity is relative to existing schemas.", "trigger": "diagnosis names high intrinsic load without naming the target learner's prior knowledge", "feedback": "Intrinsic load depends on element interactivity relative to the learner. State who the learner is; an expert's schemas chunk elements and lower the load.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "ail606_supplement_1_3_clt_reduce_load_means_remove_content", "pattern": "reduce_load_by_deleting_content", "description": "Assuming the only way to lower intrinsic load is to delete essential content, rather than sequencing or pre-training.", "trigger": "learner proposes removing essential steps to make a complex task easier", "feedback": "Manage intrinsic load with pre-training, segmenting, and sequencing so fewer novel elements interact at once, without deleting essential content.", "rail_action": "ask"}]}